{ SQLite 3.54.1 in pure paslang: the library itself, its file format,
  its SQL and its C interface, ported from SQLite's own sources and
  documents (docs/ref/sqlite; docs/SQLITE.md is the study), with no C
  library under it. The file (fileformat2.html) is SQLite's to the byte:
  a database this unit writes is one the sqlite3 program and every other
  SQLite read, and the other way, in rollback-journal and WAL mode, with
  SQLite's POSIX locks (os_unix.c) so that the two share a file at once.
  The parts, in SQLite's own order: the OS layer (os_unix.c), the page
  cache (pcache.c, pcache1.c), the pager and its journal (pager.c), the
  write-ahead log (wal.c), the b-trees (btree.c), the virtual machine
  (vdbe*.c), the sorter (vdbesort.c), the parser (parse.y through the
  tables lemon makes, P404), the code generator (select.c, where*.c,
  expr.c, insert.c, update.c, delete.c, build.c, trigger.c, fkey.c,
  alter.c, window.c, pragma.c, analyze.c, vacuum.c, attach.c, vtab.c),
  the functions (func.c, date.c, json.c, printf.c), backup.c, memdb.c,
  complete.c, table.c, vdbeblob.c, vdbetrace.c, main.c and status.c.
  SQLite's C interface is here under its own names (sqlite3_open_v2,
  sqlite3_prepare_v2, sqlite3_step, sqlite3_column_text, ...) on the
  classes TSqlite3, TSqlite3Stmt, TSqlite3Context, TSqlite3Value; a text
  is a string, a NULL text '' (with an IsNull where C tells them apart),
  a callback a routine value with an object for C's void*. TSqlite is the
  same connection as a class whose errors raise ESqliteError. A
  connection is serialized as SQLite's default build keeps it: its mutex,
  recursive, parks the pasroutine that waits for it. It is a library
  unit, not a unit of the core (P418): a program names it, uses sqlite,
  and one that does not neither sees its names nor carries its code. }
unit sqlite;

interface

uses pasobject, pasroutines;

const
